    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d394">

                <pb facs="0581" n="295" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg295"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2925</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-10-09T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 9, 1916, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 10, 1.35 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4450. I learn that one of the most forcible arguments against a resumption of
                    reckless submarine warfare advanced by Helfferich in his speech, mentioned in my
                        4417,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d394-f1"/> October 2, 6 p. m., was that a break
                    with the United States and the resulting combined pressure of Great Britain and
                    the United States on the neutral countries contiguous to Germany would force an
                    effective closing of those frontiers to the supplies which still enter Germany
                    in certain quantities.</p>
                <p>The report published in the <hi rendition="#italic">Lokal-Anzeiger</hi> yesterday
                    that the Party of the Center had split on the submarine issue and that the
                    majority would vote for submarine warfare has been officially denied by the
                    party organ. If the submarine issue comes to a vote in the Reichstag, the
                    indications are still that the Chancellor will have a majority, but even if a
                    contrary vote should be passed, it now seems improbable that the policy of the
                    Government would be altered at present.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
